WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:06.277
Dinner was over, but Quinn was still sitting at the kitchen table, talking to Mom and Grandpa.

2
00:00:06.278 --> 00:00:12.245
"What was the most boring thing that happened at school today?" Grandpa asked teasingly.

3
00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:16.401
Quinn was eager to complain about math class.

4
00:00:16.402 --> 00:00:22.812
"My teacher gave us the most boring assignment ever—Statistics and You.

5
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:29.417
"We have to use two different kinds of graphs to illustrate statistics that affect our lives.

6
00:00:29.418 --> 00:00:36.689
I don't know any statistics, never mind some that affect me. Where do I start?"

7
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:44.317
"You could start right here," Grandpa suggested. "Why am I having dinner with you?"

8
00:00:44.318 --> 00:00:50.485
"Because you're staying with us temporarily after falling and breaking your wrist."

9
00:00:50.486 --> 00:01:00.745
Grandpa nodded. "Now take out your phone and look up some statistics on older Americans and falls."

10
00:01:00.746 --> 00:01:05.009
Quinn quickly found an article online and read part of it aloud.

11
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:11.982
"'Every year one in four Americans aged 65 and over falls.

12
00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:17.945
These 29 million falls lead to 27 thousand deaths.'

13
00:01:17.946 --> 00:01:25.621
That's a lot of falls, but how do these statistics affect me? I'm not over 65."

14
00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:29.777
"Where are you sleeping tonight?" Grandpa asked.

15
00:01:29.778 --> 00:01:36.316
"On the living room couch because you're sleeping in my bed . . . Oh, I get it!

16
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:40.062
You're one of those older Americans who fell.

17
00:01:40.063 --> 00:01:46.245
The statistics are about seniors, but their problems affect their families too."

18
00:01:46.246 --> 00:01:50.731
"Exactly." Grandpa turned to Mom.

19
00:01:50.732 --> 00:01:56.588
"When my wrist heals, I should take a class at your fitness center to improve my balance."

20
00:01:56.589 --> 00:02:02.347
"That's a great idea," she said, "if we're still open.

21
00:02:02.348 --> 00:02:07.168
People used to exercise when they got off work at the candy factory.

22
00:02:07.169 --> 00:02:12.496
But business has declined since the factory shut down two months ago."

23
00:02:12.497 --> 00:02:18.693
Quinn realized that her life would definitely be affected if Mom's fitness center went out of business.

24
00:02:18.694 --> 00:02:24.057
"Hey, Mom, do you keep track of how many clients work out every day?"

25
00:02:24.058 --> 00:02:26.687
"Yes," Mom said.

26
00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:36.028
"I just got an idea for my math project, but I need to know how many people have used the center in the past six months."

27
00:02:36.029 --> 00:02:41.003
A few nights later, Quinn delivered a presentation at the kitchen table.

28
00:02:41.004 --> 00:02:46.461
"This is a single-line graph," she announced, holding up a paper.

29
00:02:46.462 --> 00:02:52.128
"It shows the number of people who have used the fitness center in the past six months.

30
00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:56.085
The figures are fairly constant for the first four months.

31
00:02:56.086 --> 00:03:01.920
But once the factory closes, they drop by 23 percent."

32
00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:09.939
Mom looked worried. "And I know new memberships are down too!"

33
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.953
"But there's also some promising news," Quinn said.

34
00:03:13.954 --> 00:03:18.635
"The number of older Americans is growing.

35
00:03:18.636 --> 00:03:24.363
That's partly due to improved medical care, which has boosted life expectancy.

36
00:03:24.364 --> 00:03:31.345
Only 42 percent of babies born in 1900 could expect to live past 65.

37
00:03:31.346 --> 00:03:38.078
But for people born in 2000, that number is projected to almost double."

38
00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:43.193
"So there are more old people now," Grandpa said.

39
00:03:43.194 --> 00:03:46.069
"How is that good news?"

40
00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.250
"Compare these two pie charts," Quinn said.

41
00:03:49.251 --> 00:03:57.531
"The U.S. Census Bureau collects and compiles lots of statistics, so I looked up some census data for our town.

42
00:03:57.532 --> 00:04:05.808
Like the rest of the U.S., people over 65 made up around 12 percent of the population in 2000.

43
00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:13.389
But now that number has increased to 15 percent, and it's expected to grow even more."

44
00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:19.378
"Maybe I should refocus my business for older members," Mom said.

45
00:04:19.379 --> 00:04:26.170
"Besides working on balance, I could offer strength-training, stretching, and aerobic exercises."

46
00:04:26.171 --> 00:04:31.661
"You could conduct a survey to see if people are interested!" Quinn suggested.

47
00:04:31.662 --> 00:04:36.288
"Oh, seniors are definitely interested," Grandpa said.

48
00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:43.983
"My friend Jim says his walking group can't meet during winter because the park's paths get icy.

49
00:04:43.984 --> 00:04:48.529
So his group would love senior fitness classes too."

50
00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:54.651
"That's what my math teacher calls anecdotal evidence, Grandpa," Quinn said.

51
00:04:54.652 --> 00:05:00.567
"You're drawing conclusions based only on your opinion—and Jim's."

52
00:05:00.568 --> 00:05:03.443
Grandpa pretended to be angry.

53
00:05:03.444 --> 00:05:09.541
"I've known Jim since high school! We're really smart guys!"

54
00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:18.330
Quinn laughed. "Yes, but you two are from the same segment of the population.

55
00:05:18.331 --> 00:05:22.314
You're the exact same age, and you're both men.

56
00:05:22.315 --> 00:05:31.108
It would be better to hear from a wider demographic through a random survey including people of different ages and genders.

57
00:05:31.109 --> 00:05:35.748
This would give Mom a more representative sample of the senior community."

58
00:05:35.749 --> 00:05:43.707
"I could draw up the questions," Mom said, "but how will we obtain a representative sample?"

59
00:05:43.708 --> 00:05:46.898
"That's easy," Grandpa said.

60
00:05:46.899 --> 00:05:54.348
"Everyone eats at the Supreme Diner on Tuesdays because it's Senior Discount Day.

61
00:05:54.349 --> 00:06:01.707
The diner's next door to your fitness center, so I could survey people passing by.

62
00:06:01.708 --> 00:06:09.473
Plus there are seniors of all ages and genders—that's a wider demographic."

63
00:06:09.474 --> 00:06:16.025
Quinn nodded. "After you've gathered the data, I could help Mom interpret it.

64
00:06:16.026 --> 00:06:30.298
And if my teacher gives me extra credit for that, statistics could have a positive effect on my math grade!"

